"Disrupting malicious uses of AI: October 2025 | OpenAI (https://openai.com/global-affairs/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-october-2025/)\nciteturn2663view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/global-affairs/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-october-2025/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 127\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: October 7, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Global Affairs L16: # Disrupting malicious uses of AI: October 2025\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Our mission is to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity. We advance this mission by deploying innovations that help people solve difficult problems and by building democratic AI grounded in common-sense rules that protect people from real harms.\nL23: Since we began our public threat reporting in cite4†February 2024 , we’ve disrupted and reported over 40 networks that violated our usage policies. This includes preventing uses of AI by authoritarian regimes to control populations or coerce other states, as well as abuses like scams, malicious cyber activity, and covert influence operations.\nL24: In this update, we share case studies from the past quarter and how we’re detecting and disrupting malicious use of our models. We continue to see threat actors bolt AI onto old playbooks to move faster, not gain novel offensive capability from our models. When activity violates our policies, we ban accounts and, where appropriate, share insights with partners.\nL25: Our public reporting, policy enforcement, and collaboration with peers aim to raise awareness of abuse while improving protections for everyday users.\nL26:   * cite5†Read the full report(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com L27: \nL28:   * cite6†Alignment L29:   * cite7†Policies and Procedures L30:   * cite8†2025 L31: ## Authors\nL32: \nL33: Ben Nimmo, Kimo Bumanglag, Michael Flossman, Nathaniel Hartley, Jack Stubbs, Albert Zhang\nL34: ## Keep reading\nL35: \nL36: cite9†View all L37: \nL38: cite10†Helping small businesses put AI to work Global AffairsSep 30, 2026 L39: \nL40: cite11†OpenAI extends cyber access to Ukraine for civilian defense Global AffairsSep 23, 2026 L41: \nL42: cite12†Sam Altman’s remarks at the United Nations Security Council Global AffairsSep 23, 2026 L43: \nL44: Research\nL45: \nL46:   * cite13†Research Index L47:   * cite14†Research Overview L48:   * cite15†Economic Research L49: \nL50: Latest Advancements\nL51: \nL52:   * cite16†GPT-6.1 Sol L53:   * cite17†GPT-6 Astra L54:   * cite18†GPT-5.6 L55:   * cite19†GPT-5.5 L56: \nL57: Safety\nL58:   * cite20†Safety Approach L59:   * cite21†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L60:   * cite22†Security & Privacy L61:   * cite23†Trust & Transparency L62: \nL63: Products\nL64:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L65:   * cite24†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L66:   * cite25†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L67:   * cite26†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L68:   * cite27†Codex†chatgpt.com L69:   * cite28†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L70:   * cite29†Release Notes L71: \nL72: API Platform\nL73: \nL74:   * cite30†Overview L75:   * cite31†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L76:   * cite32†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L77: \nL78: Business\nL79:   * cite33†Overview L80:   * cite34†Solutions L81:   * cite35†Resources L82:   * cite36†Plugins L83:   * c